# International Economic Institutions: The Bretton Woods System and Beyond

> The world economy is a machine with no single driver — so after two
> world wars and a depression, nations built institutions to steady
> it: a bank for reconstruction, a fund for currencies, later an
> organisation for trade. Knowing who does what — and who criticises
> whom — is passport knowledge for any exam on the global economy.

## 1. Why the Institutions Exist

Between the wars, the world practised economic nationalism: currency
devaluations to steal exports, tariffs to shut out imports, and
collapsing trade. The lesson drawn in 1944 at **Bretton Woods, New
Hampshire** (44 nations, Keynes and White chief among the architects)
was that beggar-thy-neighbour policies beggar everyone. The response
was a rule-bound international order: exchange rates pegged to the
dollar, the dollar convertible to gold, and three institutions to
hold the system up.

## 2. The IMF: Firefighter of the Monetary System

[definition]
The **International Monetary Fund (est. 1944, HQ Washington D.C.,
190+ members)** promotes international monetary cooperation and exchange
rate stability, and lends to countries in balance-of-payments
distress.
[/definition]

How it works:

- Members pay **quotas** (subscriptions) that fund lending; quota
  size sets voting weight, access limits and SDR allocations.
- A country short of foreign exchange borrows under programs — from
  simple **stand-by arrangements** to the **Extended Fund Facility**
  — and repays with interest.
- Loans above the first tranche carry **conditionality**: policy
  adjustments (fiscal tightening, currency reform, structural change)
  designed to restore viability — and the source of the Fund's
  controversy.

The **Special Drawing Right (SDR)** is the IMF's reserve asset, a
basket of five currencies (USD, EUR, CNY, JPY, GBP); allocated to
members, it supplements reserves — famously expanded by $650 billion
in 2021 during the pandemic. The **World Economic Outlook** is its
flagship publication.

Criticisms: austerity-heavy conditionality blamed for deepening
recessions (Asian crisis 1997–98); quota shares that under-weight
emerging economies (hence the 2010 reform, still unfinished for the
US to ratify fully); and lender-of-last-resort moral hazard.

## 3. The World Bank Group: Long-Term Development Finance

Born as the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development
(IBRD) to rebuild war-torn Europe, the **World Bank Group** now funds
development in low- and middle-income countries. Its five arms:

| Arm | Role |
| --- | --- |
| IBRD | loans to middle-income governments |
| IDA (1960) | interest-free credit to the poorest countries |
| IFC | private-sector investment in developing markets |
| MIGA | guarantees against political risk for investors |
| ICSID | arbitration of investment disputes |

Flagship knowledge products: the **World Development Report** and
the annual **Doing Business** series (discontinued 2021). Voting is
share-weighted (the United States holds a veto-scale share in IBRD).
Criticisms: conditionality, environmental and displacement costs of
large projects, and governance tilted toward rich shareholders.

## 4. The WTO: Rules for Trade

[definition]
The **World Trade Organisation (est. 1995, HQ Geneva, 164 members)**
replaced the GATT (1947) as the legal and institutional foundation of
world trade: it administers agreements, hosts negotiations
(“rounds”) and — its innovation — settles disputes with binding
rulings.
[/definition]

Core principles:

1. **Non-discrimination** — **most-favoured-nation (MFN)**: any
   concession to one member must be extended to all; **national
   treatment**: imports are taxed no worse than domestic goods once
   inside the border.
2. **Tariff binding** — negotiated ceilings, listed in schedules.
3. **Transparency** — notified policies, published rules.
4. **Dispute settlement** — panel, Appellate Body, authorised
   retaliation (the Appellate Body has been paralysed since 2019 by
   blocked appointments).

Milestones: the Uruguay Round (1995) created the WTO and brought
services (GATS) and intellectual property (TRIPS) under its roof;
the Doha Round (2001–) stalled over agriculture; the 2013 **Bali
trade facilitation** deal was its later success. India's strategic
stance — food-security stockholding and the permanent solution
debate, special-and-differential treatment — is a favourite exam
angle.

## 5. Who Runs What — Quick Comparison

| | IMF | World Bank | WTO |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Founded | 1944 | 1944 | 1995 (GATT 1947) |
| HQ | Washington D.C. | Washington D.C. | Geneva |
| Mandate | monetary stability, BoP lending | long-run development finance | trade rules |
| Chief product | WEO | World Development Report | trade agreements |
| Membership | 190+ | 189 (IBRD) | 164+ |
| Governance | quota-weighted | share-weighted | one member, one vote (consensus) |

## 6. The Institutions and India

India is a founding member of all three. It has drawn IMF support in
crises (1981 EFF, 1991 stabilization — the reform-triggering
episode) and is now a quota-rising emerging shareholder. It is among
the World Bank's largest borrowers (infrastructure, skills, health)
and IDA's biggest recipient historically. At the WTO, India anchors
the developing-country coalition — public stockholding for food
security, the permanent solution, fisheries subsidies and the
e-commerce moratorium — balancing its food-security imperatives with
its services-export ambitions.

## 7. Modern Debates

- **Legitimacy**: share-weighted voting versus one-country-one-vote
  ideals; unmet reform promises push alternatives (New Development
  Bank, AIIB, bilateral swaps).
- **Climate**: both Fund and Bank now fold climate into lending —
  the Bank's "livable planet" mission rewrite and the 2023-24
  quota-and-resource debates.
- **Geo-economics**: industrial policy, friend-shoring and export
  controls test the WTO's disciplines; its appellate paralysis moves
  disputes into the open (the Multi-Party Interim Appeal arrangement
  and unilateral retaliation).
- **Debt distress**: the Common Framework for debt treatment tries to
  coordinate the IMF-World Bank system with new creditors, China
  first among them.

## 8. Summary

The Bretton Woods twins — IMF for the short-run plumbing of payments,
World Bank for long-run development finance — plus the WTO's
rulebook for trade, make up the triad that keeps the world economy
coordinated. Each has a distinctive mandate, governance quirk and
criticism attached; each has been reshaped by crisis and is being
reshaped again by the return of geopolitics. Exams probe the division
of labour — who lends for what, founded when, governed how — and the
critiques that have followed each institution since 1944.
